    "question": {
      "math_academy_id": "q-141527",
      "knowledge_point_id": "ec6076ff-1538-5bc0-973d-33d908b0b4d2",
      "knowledge_point": "Completing the Square on a Quadratic Expression",
      "problem": "Complete the square on the quadratic expression ${y}^{2}+11y+25.$",
      "worked_solution": "First, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}+2\\cdot \\frac{11}{2}y+25\n$$\n\nNow, here's the trick. We *add* and *then subtract* ${(\\frac{11}{2})}^{2},$ writing the constant term $25$ last:\n\n$$\n{{y}^{2}+2\\cdot \\frac{11}{2}y+{(\\frac{11}{2})}^{2}}_{⏟}-{(\\frac{11}{2})}^{2}+25\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n{(y+\\frac{11}{2})}^{2}-{(\\frac{11}{2})}^{2}+25\n$$\n\nFinally, evaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n{(y+\\frac{11}{2})}^{2}-\\frac{21}{4}.\n$$",
      "answer_fields": [
