    "question": {
      "math_academy_id": "q-35079",
      "knowledge_point_id": "3202d7cc-541b-553b-8ec1-d4ea85dd0590",
      "knowledge_point": "Completing the Square on an Expression With a Negative Leading Coefficient",
      "problem": "Rewrite $-2{z}^{2}-14z+16$ by completing the square.",
      "worked_solution": "Notice that the leading coefficient is $-2.$ So, we take out a factor of $-2$ from the entire expression:\n\n$$\n-2({z}^{2}+7z-8)\n$$\n\nLet's now complete the square for the expression inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n{z}^{2}+7z-8\n$$\n\nFirst, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{z}^{2}+2\\cdot \\frac{7}{2}\\cdot z-8\n$$\n\nThen, we *add* and *then subtract* ${(\\frac{7}{2})}^{2},$ writing the constant term $8$ last:\n\n$$\n{{z}^{2}+2\\cdot \\frac{7}{2}\\cdot z+{(\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}}_{⏟}-{(\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}-8\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n{(z+\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}-{(\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}-8\n$$\n\nEvaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n{(z+\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}-\\frac{81}{4}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute this result back into our original expression::\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2({z}^{2}+7z-8) & =-2({(z+\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}-\\frac{81}{4}) \\\\  & =-2{(z+\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}+(-2)\\cdot (-\\frac{81}{4}) \\\\  & =-2{(z+\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}+\\frac{81}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
